The buggy address belongs to the object at ff11000000144000
 which belongs to the cache kmalloc-2k of size 2048
The buggy address is located 52 bytes inside of
 freed 2048-byte region [ff11000000144000, ff11000000144800)
==================================================================

So the freed object is the block map allocated by dbMount() in
fs/jfs/jfs_dmap.c, and it is read 52 bytes in while resolving a name in
the mounted filesystem.  The report does not contain the free stack (the
slab page had already been recycled), but the bmap is what the map teardown
(dbUnmount()) frees, so the lookup is racing with, or happening after, the
map teardown.

How the fuzzer reaches it
-------------------------

The crashing program mounts a crafted 16 MiB JFS image read-only
(MS_RDONLY|MS_NOATIME with uid/gid/quota/errors=remount-ro/nodiscard
options), then opens a file inside it.  The crashing open() is on a path
whose mount point was moved around with RENAME_EXCHANGE beforehand:

  syz_mount_image$jfs(...)                            # mount image on ./file0
  mount$overlay(...)                                  # (fails)
  openat("./file1")
  renameat2("./file1", "./file0", RENAME_EXCHANGE)    # swap, moving the mount
  openat("./file1")                                   # <- crash in here

Reproducer
----------

syzbot's automated reproducer extraction failed for this crash (18 minutes
of attempts, single occurrence), so the exact interleaving is not pinned
down yet.  The crafted image is attached below in case it is useful; to
mount it by hand:

  base64 -d jfs_diRead.zlib.b64 > jfs.zlib
  python3 -c "import zlib;open('jfs.img','wb').write(zlib.decompress(open('jfs.zlib','rb').read()))"
  mount -t jfs -o loop,ro,uid=0,gid=0,quota,errors=remount-ro,nodiscard jfs.img /mnt/t

